{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>If the men have come to call you.</b> There are two kinds of agency. Sometimes the agent stipulates with the man he is going after, saying: 'on this condition I escort you on the road you travel' — that you must promise me you will fulfill my request. And sometimes the agent stipulates nothing at all, but performs only the agency of summoning, even though they do not promise him to fulfill his word; and there, weighty matters will be discussed — whether the matter is worthy of being done or not. And thus HaShem said to Balaam: if these men wish to make a firm arrangement and a final deal with you, to guarantee you such-and-such silver and gold so that you too will guarantee them to curse — then 'you shall not go with them,' for the matter is not in your authority. But if they did not come for that, but 'if to call you' alone, and the matter will be in your authority to do or not — then 'rise, go with them.' And this is what He said: 'but the word…' etc." "And since I have seen in this passage much variation regarding the matter of the emissaries — for he calls them sometimes 'the princes of Moab,' sometimes 'the princes of Balak,' and sometimes 'men' — and there is also a great difficulty in what is written: 'And the anger of HaShem was kindled because he was going,' after He had given him permission, 'rise, go with them' — I say that there were two kinds of emissaries there: the princes of Balak, who are the ministers specially appointed to the king, and these he called 'men,' a term of importance, for a king's servant is like a king; and 'the princes of Moab' are the princes of the province who own the land, whereas Balak had only the kingship and not the land, for they appointed him king only temporarily. And therefore, for two reasons, the princes of Balak did not pursue the curses of Balaam as eagerly as the princes of Moab did. The first reason: since the princes of Moab possess the whole land, whereas Balak and his princes were appointed only temporarily — therefore surely the princes of Moab were more insistent upon their deliverance through this curse than the princes of Balak. And furthermore, since the princes of Balak were important men, they would not seek so much from Balaam that he should curse against the will of the blessed El." "And with this proposal all the difficulties are resolved, for in every mission both the princes of Balak and the princes of Moab went, and he called all of them 'the elders of Moab.' 'And they came to Balaam and spoke to him the words of Balak'; and when he answered them, 'Lodge here this night,' and dismissed them, it is written: 'And the princes of Moab sat with Balaam' — for they, being the principals, sat to await his response, but the princes of Balak were not so insistent and did not sit with him. 'And Elohim came to Balaam and said: Who are these men with you?' — we have already said that wherever he mentions 'the men,' he is speaking of the princes of Balak, and the proof is from what is written after the incident of the she-ass: 'Rise, go with the men,' and it is written, 'And Balaam went with the princes of Balak.' And likewise the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Who are these men with you?' — and the word מִי functions as an expression of 'וְכִי' ('is it really?'), meaning: are the important men with you? And why did they not sit with you, but only the lesser ones? And he answered: 'Balak son of Zippor, king of Moab, sent to me' — therefore these princes who are from Moab, with whom the matter directly concerns, sit with me and wait, but the princes of Balak have no such stake in the matter. 'And Balaam rose in the morning and said to the princes of Balak: HaShem refuses' — and he did not say so to the princes of Moab, so as not to distress them face to face. Or one may say: he said even to the princes of Balak, the important ones, 'HaShem refuses to let me go with you' — but would go with princes more important than you, and all the more so with the princes of Moab. 'And the princes of Moab rose and came to Balak' etc., for they are the principals, but the princes of Balak were not insistent about returning. 'And He said to him: If the men have come to call you' — the important ones, the princes of Balak — 'rise, go with them,' precisely, for there is no concern that they will entice you on the road to act against the will of El. And He permitted him to go specifically with the important men, the princes of Balak, and not with the lesser princes of Moab, for there is reason to fear that perhaps along the whole road sinners will entice you and lead you to transgress the will of your Maker. And it is written: 'And Balaam rose in the morning' etc. 'and went with the princes of Moab' — contrary to the will of the blessed El, for He had not permitted him to go except with the important men; therefore 'the anger of HaShem was kindled because he was going.' And after the incident of the she-ass, when he said, 'I have sinned' etc., the angel of HaShem said to him: 'Go with the men' — that is, the princes of Balak — and so he did. And this is what it says: 'And Balaam went with the princes of Balak.' And with this everything is settled."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:01.249541933Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}